SUPPLY CHAIN MORNING
Issue 299 | Wednesday, February 25, 2026

Good morning. Three stories and a number.

1) Transpacific spot rates slip for a fourth week
Shanghai to Los Angeles spot pricing fell another 6% to $1,910 per FEU, the lowest since November. Carriers blanked eleven sailings for the first half of March, which is more than they blanked for all of February, and it still has not put a floor under the market. Contract season starts in earnest next week; shippers we spoke with are going in asking for flat.

2) The FMC's detention and demurrage billing rule is now fully in force
As of yesterday, carriers and marine terminals must issue invoices within 30 days and include the thirteen data elements the Commission finalized last year, or the invoice is not enforceable. The trade groups say most carriers are compliant; two forwarders told us the same three carriers are still sending invoices with the container number and nothing else.

3) Class 8 orders fall to a 24-month low
January orders came in at 14,800 units, down 31% year on year. Fleets are running trucks longer and waiting on the emissions-rule outcome before committing to 2027 model-year equipment. Used truck prices ticked up for the first time in eighteen months, which tells you where the demand went.

NUMBER OF THE DAY: 1.38. The US inventory-to-sales ratio for December, unchanged from November and still above the pre-2020 norm. Nobody is destocking, nobody is restocking.

That's the morning. Reply with tips; we read all of them.

